{0 Streaming}

The streaming API lets you parse input that isn't fully available upfront: files,
network sockets, pipes, or any byte source. The same parser code works with both
{!val:Parseff.parse} (strings) and {!val:Parseff.parse_source} (streams), with
no changes needed.

{1 [parse_source]}

{!val:Parseff.parse_source} runs a parser pulling input from a
{!type:Parseff.Source.t} on demand. Behaves identically to {!val:Parseff.parse}:
same parsers, same result type, same error handling.

{@ocaml skip[val parse_source :
  ?max_depth:int ->
  ?backtrack_window:int ->
  ?consume:[ `All | `Partial ] ->
  ?diagnostics:'d Diagnostics.t ->
  Source.t ->
  (unit -> 'a) ->
  ('a, error) Stdlib.result]}

{@ocaml skip[
  let ic = open_in "data.json" in
  let source = Parseff.Source.of_channel ic in
  let result = Parseff.parse_source source json in
  close_in ic;
  result
]}

Error positions are absolute offsets from the start of the stream, also after
Parseff dropped old input to keep the buffer bounded. An error does not keep the
dropped input alive: failures that are older than the buffered input still count
for the position of the error, but the buffer does not grow for them.

The [~max_depth] parameter works the same as in {!val:Parseff.parse}. The
[~backtrack_window] parameter bounds how much buffered input Parseff may retain
for backtracking and in-flight streaming operations. It defaults to 65536 bytes.

{1 Reading the whole source}

By default {!val:Parseff.parse_source} fails with [Expected End_of_input] when
input remains after the parser returns, as {!val:Parseff.end_of_input} does. The
check pulls one more byte from the source. It does not read the rest of the
source: it stops at the first byte that remains.

To parse a leading part of the source and leave the rest unread, pass
[~consume:`Partial]:

{@ocaml skip[Parseff.parse_source ~consume:`Partial source parser]}

[~diagnostics] collects the diagnostics of the parse. See {!page:diagnostics}.

{@ocaml skip[
  let ic = open_in "data.json" in
  let source = Parseff.Source.of_channel ic in
  let result = Parseff.parse_source source json in
  close_in ic;
  result
]}

{1 Many values from one source}

{!val:Parseff.parse_source_each} parses a stream of values, such as NDJSON
lines or log records, from one source. Each element of the sequence runs the
parser once on the rest of the source. Use it instead of one
{!val:Parseff.parse_source} call with {!val:Parseff.many} when the consumer
must get each value as it arrives.

{@ocaml skip[val parse_source_each :
  ?max_depth:int ->
  ?backtrack_window:int ->
  ?diagnostics:'d Diagnostics.t ->
  ?trace:(trace_event -> unit) ->
  ?pending:(unit -> bool) ->
  Source.t ->
  (unit -> 'a) ->
  ('a, error) Stdlib.result Seq.t]}

The parser reads one value and the separator after it:

{@ocaml[
let record () =
  let id = Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 (fun c -> c >= '0' && c <= '9') in
  let _ = Parseff.char '\n' in
  int_of_string id

let () =
  Parseff.parse_source_each (Parseff.Source.of_string "1\n22\n333\n") record
  |> Seq.iter (function
       | Ok id -> Printf.printf "%d\n" id
       | Error e -> prerr_endline (Parseff.render_error e))
]}

A separator parser that reads past the value, such as
{!val:Parseff.skip_whitespace}, holds that value back until more input or the
end of input arrives. The sequence is ephemeral: force each element once, in
order.

{1 The Source module}

{!module:Parseff.Source} wraps a readable byte stream behind a uniform interface.
Five constructors cover the common cases, ordered here from simplest to most
low-level.

{2 [Source.of_string]}

{!val:Parseff.Source.of_string} creates a source from a complete string. Useful
for testing streaming code paths with known input:

{@ocaml skip[val of_string : string -> Source.t]}

{@ocaml skip[
  let source = Parseff.Source.of_string "[1, 2, 3]" in
  Parseff.parse_source source json_array
]}

{2 [Source.of_channel]}

{!val:Parseff.Source.of_channel} creates a source that reads from an
[in_channel]. The [~buf_size] parameter controls the internal read buffer
(default: 4096 bytes).

{@ocaml skip[val of_channel : ?buf_size:int -> in_channel -> Source.t]}

{@ocaml skip[
  let ic = open_in "large_dataset.json" in
  let source = Parseff.Source.of_channel ~buf_size:8192 ic in
  let result = Parseff.parse_source source json in
  close_in ic;
  result
]}

A larger buffer means fewer system calls but more memory. For most files, the
default 4096 is fine. For multi-megabyte files, 64KB or larger reduces overhead.

{2 [Source.of_chunks]}

{!val:Parseff.Source.of_chunks} creates a source from a function that returns
string chunks. Return [Some s] with a non-empty string for data, or [None] to
signal EOF. Empty strings ([Some ""]) are silently skipped.

{@ocaml skip[val of_chunks : (unit -> string option) -> Source.t]}

This is the recommended way to wrap stateful or callback-based data sources.
The library handles buffer management internally — no manual [Bytes.blit_string],
position tracking, or [min] calculations needed.

[of_chunks] has no read buffer. Parseff copies each chunk into its input. When
it keeps no earlier input and takes the whole chunk, the chunk becomes the input
with no copy. Thus a chunk must not change after the function returns it. This
matters only for a string made with [Bytes.unsafe_to_string] from bytes that are
written again.

{@ocaml skip[
  (* Eio promise — await once, then signal EOF *)
  let fetched = ref false in
  let source = Parseff.Source.of_chunks (fun () ->
      if !fetched then None
      else begin
        fetched := true;
        Some (Promise.await_exn promise)
      end) in
  Parseff.parse_source source json
]}

{@ocaml skip[
  (* HTTP body arriving in pieces *)
  let source = Parseff.Source.of_chunks (fun () ->
      match Http.Body.read body with
      | `Data chunk -> Some chunk
      | `Eof -> None) in
  Parseff.parse_source source my_parser
]}

Compare with the equivalent {!val:Parseff.Source.of_function} version, which
requires the caller to manage a position cursor, calculate byte counts, and blit
into a pre-allocated buffer:

{@ocaml skip[
  (* Same Eio example with of_function — more boilerplate *)
  let data = ref None in
  let pos = ref 0 in
  let source = Parseff.Source.of_function (fun buf off len ->
      let s = match !data with
        | Some s -> s
        | None ->
            let s = Promise.await_exn promise in
            data := Some s; s
      in
      let remaining = String.length s - !pos in
      let n = min len remaining in
      Bytes.blit_string s !pos buf off n;
      pos := !pos + n;
      n) in
  Parseff.parse_source source json
]}

{2 [Source.of_seq]}

{!val:Parseff.Source.of_seq} creates a source from a lazy sequence of string
chunks. Each element is a chunk of input; the sequence ending ([Seq.Nil]) signals
EOF. It reads chunks as [of_chunks] does, so a chunk must not change after the
sequence returns it.

{@ocaml skip[val of_seq : string Seq.t -> Source.t]}

{@ocaml skip[
  (* From a list of chunks *)
  let source = Parseff.Source.of_seq
    (List.to_seq ["{ \"key\""; ": "; "\"value\" }"]) in
  Parseff.parse_source source json
]}

[of_seq] is useful when the data is already structured as a sequence — for
example, reading lines from a file or splitting a large string into fixed-size
pieces:

{@ocaml skip[
  (* Fixed-size chunks from a string, useful for testing *)
  let chunk_seq ?(chunk_size = 4096) input =
    let len = String.length input in
    let rec go pos () =
      if pos >= len then Seq.Nil
      else
        let n = min chunk_size (len - pos) in
        Seq.Cons (String.sub input pos n, go (pos + n))
    in
    go 0

  let source = Parseff.Source.of_seq (chunk_seq ~chunk_size:2 "hello world")
]}

{2 [Source.of_function]}

{!val:Parseff.Source.of_function} creates a source from a low-level read
function. The function signature matches POSIX [read(2)]: [read buf off len]
fills [buf] starting at [off] with up to [len] bytes and returns the count
actually written. Return [0] to signal EOF.

{@ocaml skip[val of_function : ?initial_buffer_size:int -> (bytes -> int -> int -> int) -> Source.t]}

This is the low-level escape hatch for byte sources that natively fill a
pre-allocated buffer. For most use cases, prefer {!val:Parseff.Source.of_chunks}
or {!val:Parseff.Source.of_seq} which handle buffer management automatically.

{@ocaml skip[
  let unix_file_descriptor = Parseff.Source.of_function (fun buf off len ->
    Unix.read fd buf off len
  ) in
  Parseff.parse_source source my_parser

  let decompression_stream = Parseff.Source.of_function (fun buf off len ->
    Zlib.inflate zstream buf off len
  )

  let network_socket = Parseff.Source.of_function (fun buf off len ->
    Unix.recv socket buf off len []
  )
]}

The [~initial_buffer_size] parameter sets the first size of the read buffer, which is the [len]
of the first [read] call (default: 2040 bytes). After a read that fills the
buffer, the next read gets a buffer of 4096 bytes, or of [initial_buffer_size]
bytes when that is larger. The larger buffer is allocated only when that read happens.
A source made for one short message, such as an HTTP request head, allocates
less with an [initial_buffer_size] near the size of the message:

{@ocaml skip[
  let request_head = Parseff.Source.of_function ~initial_buffer_size:512 (fun buf off len ->
    Unix.read fd buf off len
  )
]}

{1 Why the same parser works for both}

Parseff uses the same parser API for both in-memory and streaming input. The
runner decides where bytes come from:

- {!val:Parseff.parse}: reads from a fixed string
- {!val:Parseff.parse_source}: reads from a {!type:Parseff.Source.t}, fetching
  more data on demand

The parser doesn't know which one it's running under. The code is identical:

{@ocaml[
  (* This parser works with both runners *)
  let number () =
    let s =
      Parseff.take_while ~at_least:1 (fun c -> c >= '0' && c <= '9') ~label:"digit"
    in
    int_of_string s

  (* From a string *)
  let _ = Parseff.parse "42" number

  (* From a source *)
  let _ =
    Parseff.parse_source (Parseff.Source.of_string "42") number
]}

In Parseff, streaming is transparent at the parser-function level: the
same parser works against a fixed string or a streaming source. The runtime
uses a bounded sliding buffer, not an append-forever buffer.

{1 How backtracking works across chunks}

When Parseff refills a source, it keeps only the region that is still needed for
backtracking. The retained window starts at the oldest active rewind point and
ends at the newest buffered byte.

Two things keep old bytes alive:

- active backtracking frames such as {!val:Parseff.or_}, {!val:Parseff.look_ahead},
  and the internal rewind points used by repetition combinators
- in-flight streaming primitives that still need earlier bytes to finish
  building a value (for example {!val:Parseff.take_while})

{2 General advice}

Most parsers become streaming parsers by changing only the runner: keep the same
parser functions and switch from {!val:Parseff.parse} to
{!val:Parseff.parse_source}. Start
there, then tune the streaming behavior only if the grammar needs it.

In practice:

- Think of [~backtrack_window] as retained undo history, not as a chunk size.
- Prefer grammars that dispatch early, so the runtime does not need to keep a
  long ambiguous prefix alive.
- Call {!val:Parseff.commit} only when a branch has become logically certain.
- If you hit [Backtrack_window_exceeded], first try to commit earlier; only
  increase [~backtrack_window] when the ambiguity is real.

The guides section includes a small CSV walkthrough that starts with a normal
string parser and only changes the runner, plus a second guide that focuses on
[commit] and [~backtrack_window] for grammars with long-lived ambiguity.

A small example:

{@ocaml[
let http_method () =
  Parseff.or_
    (fun () ->
      let _ = Parseff.consume "GET " in
      Parseff.commit ();
      `GET)
    (fun () ->
      let _ = Parseff.consume "POST " in
      Parseff.commit ();
      `POST)
    ()
]}

[commit ()] acts on the innermost frame only: the nearest {!val:Parseff.or_},
{!val:Parseff.one_of}, {!val:Parseff.many}, {!val:Parseff.sep_by},
{!val:Parseff.optional} or [fold_*] frame. It does nothing outside any frame and
inside {!val:Parseff.look_ahead}. It does not release outer frames. In
[many (fun () -> or_ a b ())], a [commit ()] inside the [or_] does not let the
[many] frame release older buffered input: [many] still keeps the input from the
start of the current iteration. To release that input, call [commit ()] in the
body of [many], outside the [or_].

{!val:Parseff.cut} seals every frame opened since the innermost enclosing
{!val:Parseff.context}, not only the nearest one. Use it when a whole construct
becomes certain: after [cut ()], none of those frames pins buffered input, and
a failure of the construct is not retried by the frames outside it. With a
small window, an [or_] whose left branch reads a long run of tokens fails with
[Backtrack_window_exceeded] until the branch calls [cut ()].

Before [commit ()], Parseff must keep enough buffered input to rewind and try
the other branch. After [commit ()], that rewind point stops pinning the old
prefix.

If the retained region would exceed [~backtrack_window], parsing fails with
[Backtrack_window_exceeded]. This is a real contract, not a best-effort hint.

{1 Thread safety}

{!val:Parseff.parse_source} (and {!val:Parseff.parse}) are safe to call from
multiple OCaml 5 domains concurrently. The state of a parse (the parser
position, internal buffer, recursion depth counter) is created inside each
call. While the parse runs, Parseff keeps that state in a slot that belongs
to the domain, so parses on different domains do not share it.

A domain runs one parse at a time. System threads ([Thread]) that share a
domain also share that slot, and the runtime can switch threads at any point
inside a parser. Two threads that parse at the same time on one domain can get
wrong results. Run each parse on its own domain, or hold one [Mutex] around
each parse call.

{@ocaml skip[
  (* Parse different files in parallel *)
  let results =
    List.init num_workers (fun i ->
      Domain.spawn (fun () ->
        let ic = open_in files.(i) in
        let source = Parseff.Source.of_channel ic in
        let result = Parseff.parse_source source json in
        close_in ic;
        result))
    |> List.map Domain.join
]}

The one constraint: do not share a single {!type:Parseff.Source.t} across
domains. Each source has mutable read state (buffer position, EOF flag), so
concurrent access would race. Create a separate source per domain.

A source is for one call of {!val:Parseff.parse_source} or
{!val:Parseff.parse_source_each}. {!val:Parseff.Source.of_string} starts again
from the beginning, but the other sources continue where the last read stopped,
and bytes that the first call read ahead are lost.

{2 Concurrent parses on one domain}

Two parses can run at the same time on one domain, for example in two Eio
fibers, when the only thing that suspends is the source [read] function. The
parse state is stored per domain and Parseff installs it again each time [read]
returns. A suspension inside a parser callback, such as a [satisfy] predicate or
a [many] body, is not supported.

This is for fibers, which switch only at [read]. It does not hold for system
threads, which can switch anywhere.

{1 Limitations}

{2 Bounded backtracking}

The buffer no longer grows forever, but a parser can still exceed the configured
[~backtrack_window] if it keeps an old rewind point alive for too long. Typical
fixes are:

- dispatch earlier (for example with {!val:Parseff.peek_char})
- call {!val:Parseff.commit} once the grammar has clearly committed to a branch
- increase [~backtrack_window] for genuinely ambiguous grammars

{2 Blocking reads}

{!val:Parseff.Source.of_channel}, {!val:Parseff.Source.of_chunks},
{!val:Parseff.Source.of_function}, and {!val:Parseff.Source.of_seq} all block the
calling thread when waiting for data. For async/event-driven architectures, run
the parse on another domain:

{@ocaml skip[
  let result = Domain.spawn (fun () ->
    Parseff.parse_source source json
  ) |> Domain.join
]}

{2 Regex at chunk boundaries}

{!val:Parseff.match_regex} reads more input when a match reaches the end of the
buffered input, because more input could change the result. A match and the byte
after it must fit in the [~backtrack_window], unless the source ends after the
match. Otherwise the parse fails with [Backtrack_window_exceeded].

A regex that needs later bytes to choose a longer match can return the shorter
one when the window is small: [a(bc)?] on ["abc;"] can return ["a"] with a
window below [6].

{2 One parse returns one result}

{!val:Parseff.parse_source} runs the parser to completion and returns a single
result. To get each value as it arrives, use {!val:Parseff.parse_source_each}.
